/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-72739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "110"] ["math" "176"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "275"] ["math" "352"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "352"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-10.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-72752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=28.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{3}{4},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{3}{4}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{3}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-21"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $3$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-3det(A) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15"] ["math" "-20"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=10.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{2},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{2}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=4.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}:=-{R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-130854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}"] ["math" "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-42076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=-108$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by multiplying the $2$nd row by $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the determinant is multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{2}$ as well.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-108) \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6b5a9b253e74076a2847b9ab0daf3e17a3604a180243e69503c146df4d8522b"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-108"] ["math" "-216"] ["math" "108"] ["math" "216"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "q-43081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "54"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/state.json" "file_sha256" "306c385ac5ee5e6bb656502f27f5d94d63080dc9f3eb91fd1c28782afcd26037"} {"question" "e-4422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4422.json" "file_sha256" "66161f30c7440ff1bd31626471f1f253a1ef23c4b18cd15bf0bfa56577dd0aa0"} {"question" "e-4422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4422.json" "file_sha256" "66161f30c7440ff1bd31626471f1f253a1ef23c4b18cd15bf0bfa56577dd0aa0"} {"question" "e-4423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4423.json" "file_sha256" "3bc94bc43c79a180b09efb21de768f534f94864038c5f5bffa5acd2db7dd0b26"} {"question" "e-4423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4423.json" "file_sha256" "3bc94bc43c79a180b09efb21de768f534f94864038c5f5bffa5acd2db7dd0b26"} {"question" "e-6148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5.$ What is the value of $\\det(2A)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-6148.json" "file_sha256" "5e411e6efc16e3a2fc62e74b164c7b44c4794073555594392b94c20e1099b134"} {"question" "e-6148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2A) & ={2}^{4}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-6148.json" "file_sha256" "5e411e6efc16e3a2fc62e74b164c7b44c4794073555594392b94c20e1099b134"} {"question" "e-4424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5,$ $R_6:= 4 R_6,$ and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7.$ What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4424.json" "file_sha256" "1b3758cf73ce19b93c5e31910a937a0881ec5ce4e310fec0750daab7950c9d0a"} {"question" "e-4424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-4424.json" "file_sha256" "1b3758cf73ce19b93c5e31910a937a0881ec5ce4e310fec0750daab7950c9d0a"} {"question" "e-6143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/assets/3692f985d592f9680ca9fbf38991df32c7665ae02c7dae04fec6062e3b62884d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-6143.json" "file_sha256" "0aad1b23cf51e2ad3fc221c8ac930137484b3badbdd24cfab021a38dcad919ca"} {"question" "e-6143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14043178/example-6143.json" "file_sha256" "0aad1b23cf51e2ad3fc221c8ac930137484b3badbdd24cfab021a38dcad919ca"}] "usage" {"e-4422" [] "q-43078" [] "e-6143" [] "q-42076" [] "q-43077" [] "e-6148" [] "q-42505" [] "e-4424" [] "e-4423" []} "basis" 1622 "retractions" [[17592186120381 :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and $det(X) = 105$. The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{3}↔R_{1}$ (switching the first and the third rows). What is the value of $det(Y)$?"] [17592186120380 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\cdot 6$ matrix and $det(A) = 35$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{1}:=-\\frac{3}{5}R_{1}$. What is the value of $det(B)$?"] [17592186120359 :question/problem "Suppose $U$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and $det(U) = 10$. The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_{2}:=R_{2} + 6R_{4}$ and $R_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{3}$. What is the value of $det(V)$?"] [17592186120347 :question/problem "Given that $det(A) = det(B)$, which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186119516 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)$?"] [17592186119516 :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= -det(A) \\\\\n&= - (-16) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119517 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?"] [17592186119517 :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= \\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119590 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times4$ matrix and $\\det(A)=5$. What is the value of $\\det(2A)$?"] [17592186119590 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k$, and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\ndet(k \\cdot A) = k^{n} \\cdot det(A)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(2A) &= 2^{4} \\cdot det(A) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119518 :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$, $R_6:= 4 R_6$, and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?"] [17592186119518 :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4$, so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row, so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= 4 \\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\\n&=-4det(A) \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119589 :question/problem "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y)$, which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119589 :question/worked-solution "Since\n\n$$\n\\det(X)=\\det(Y)\n$$\n\n, the row operation applied to $X$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form\n\n$$\nR_j:= R_j + (k)R_i, \\quad i \\neq j\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\ =\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= - 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= det(Y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + 3R_3\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118558 17592186118559 17592186118560 17592186118561 17592186118562 17592186118630 17592186118631 17592186118632 17592186118633 17592186118634 17592186118756 17592186118757 17592186118758 17592186118759 17592186118760 17592186118762 17592186118763 17592186118764 17592186118765 17592186118766] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118557 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118557 :answer-field/id] [17592186118557 :answer-field/key] [17592186118557 :answer-field/type] [17592186118629 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118629 :answer-field/id] [17592186118629 :answer-field/key] [17592186118